<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<pretext>
  <article xml:id="article">
    
    <worksheet xml:id="ws-usub">
      <title>Worksheet for Section 5.5: Integration by Substitution</title>

      <objectives>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find antiderivatives using the method of substitution.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Evaluate definite integrals using the method of substitution.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </objectives>

      <exercisegroup>
      <title>Using Substitution</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use the specified substitution to evaluate the given integrals.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-usub-1">
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int(3x-5)^{17}\,dx</m>, <m>u=3x-5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int_0^4 x\sqrt{x^2+9}\,dx</m>, <m>u=x^2 + 9</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          
          <p>
            <m>\int \frac{e^{\sqrt x}}{\sqrt x}\,dx</m>, <m>u=\sqrt{x}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle\int \frac{\cos 3x\,dx}{5 + 2 \sin 3x}</m>, <m>u=5 + 2 \sin 3x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      </exercisegroup> <exercisegroup>
      <title>Finding Substitutions</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          In these problems, you need to determine the substitution yourself.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int (4-3x)^7 \,dx</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/3} \csc^2(5x)\,dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int x^2 e^{3x^3-1}\,dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Sometimes there is more than one way to skin a cat:
          </p>
        </introduction>


        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>\int\frac{x}{1+x}\,dx</m>, using long division on the integrand.
            </p>
          </statement>

          </task>


        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>\int\frac{x}{1+x}\,dx</m> by substituting <m>u = 1+x</m>
            </p>
          </statement>

          </task>


        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>\int\frac{2z\,dz}{\sqrt[3]{z^2+1}}</m> by substituting <m>u=z^2 + 1</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>

          </task>


        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>\int\frac{2z\,dz}{\sqrt[3]{z^2+1}}</m>, by substituting <m>u=\sqrt[3]{z^2 + 1}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>

          </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercisegroup>
      <title>Integration with half-angle identities</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use the identity
          <md>
            \cos 2\alpha  =\cos^2 \alpha - \sin^2 \alpha = 2\cos^2\alpha -1 = 1- 2\sin^2 \alpha
          </md>
          to find the following:
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\int\sin^2 x\,dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>

        </exercise>



        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\int\cos^2 x\,dx</m>
            </p>
          </statement>

          </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

              <exercise workspace="1in">
        <title>Symmetry</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            Which of the following integrals is/are equal to zero?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <choices>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{1} \sin(x^3)\,dx</m>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <feedback>
              <p>
                The interval is symmetric about the origin, and the integrand is an odd function.
                So the integral is zero.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>

            <choice correct="yes">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  <m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 \sin(x^2)\,dx</m>
                </p>
              </statement>

              <feedback>
                <p>
                  The interval is symmetric about the origin, and the integrand is an odd function.
                  So the integral is zero.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>

          <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{1} x^2 e^{x^3}\,dx</m>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <feedback>
              <p>
                The interval is symmetric about the origin, but the integrand is neither even nor odd.
                The integral is equal to
                <md>
                  \left.\frac{1}{3}e^{x^3}\right|_{-1}^{1} = \frac{1}{3}(e - e^{-1}) &gt; 0.
                </md>
              </p>
            </feedback>
            </choice>

          <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{1} \sin(x^2)\,dx</m>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <feedback>
              <p>
                The interval is symmetric about the origin, but the integrand is an even function.
                The integral is equal to <m>2\int_0^1 \sin(x^2)\,dx</m>, which is positive.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find
              <md>
                \int \sec x\,dx
              </md>
              by multiplying the numerator and denominator by <m>\sec x + \tan x</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>

          </exercise>
      </worksheet>
    </article>
  </pretext>